# Cloning of DNA segments of phage 2C, which allows autonomous plasmid replication in <em>Bacillus subtilis</em>

## abstract
The chromosome of Bacillus subtilis phage 2C is a 100-MDa double-stranded DNA molecule, containing hydroxymethyluracil in place of thymine and carrying redundant ends each encompassing 10% of the genome. 2C DNA was cleaved with EcoRI and HindIII, and cloned in the shuttle plasmids pSC 540 and pCP 115, both containing segments originating from B. subtilis and Escherichia coli plasmids. These chimaerical plasmids, carrying the chloramphenicol resistance gene, were unable to replicate in B. subtilis; this ability was restored, however, after the insertion of viral DNA segments. Physical maps of the recombinant plasmids were made; a large deletion of the E. coli-derived segment of pSC 540 was observed (which paralleled a loss of replication in this host), whereas addition of 2C DNA segments in pCP 115 was not accompanied by deletion (replication in E. coli was conserved in this case). Cloned viral segments mapped mostly, but not exclusively, within the redundant ends of 2C DNA. It is suggested that the thirteen recombinant clones carried the replication origin region of phage 2C DNA, and that these sequences originated within or close to the redundant extremities of the viral chromosome.

Cloning of DNA segments of phage 2 C, which allows autonomous plasmid replication in Bacillus subtilis
Nathalie N. LANNOY, Philippe P. HOET and Carlo G. COCITO Unit of Microbiology and Genctics,Institute of Cell Pathology, University of Louvain, Medical School, Brussels
(Received January 25/April 30,1985)- EJB 85 0067
The chromosome of Bacillus subtilis phage 2C is a 100-MDa double-stranded DNA molecule,containing hydroxymethyluracil in place of thymine and carrying redundant ends each encompassing $10\%$ of the genome. 2C DNA wascleaved with EcoRI and HindII, and cloned in the shuttle plasmids pSC540 and pCP115, both containing segments originating from B. subtilis and Escherichia coli plasmids. These chimaerical plasmids, carrying the chloramphenicolresistance gene, were unable to replicate in B.subtilis;this ability was restored, however, after the insertion of viral DNA segments.Physical maps of the recombinant plasmids were made; a large deletion of the $E$ coli-derived segment of pSC 540 was observed (which paralleled a loss of replication in this host), whereas addition of 2C DNA segments in pCP115 was not accompanied by deletion (replication in $E$ + coli was conserved inthis case).Cloned viral segments mapped mostly,but not exclusively,within the redundant ends of 2C DNA. It is suggested that the thirteen recombinant clones carried the replication origin region of phage 2C DNA, and that these sequences originated withinor close to the redundant extremities of the viral chromosome.
Isolation of the replication origin of bacterial and viral chromosomes is a prerequisite for elucidating their unique property as binding sites for specific enzymes and inhibitors. Additional features of the replication origin are its dependence on both transcriptional and translational specific polymerization processes, and its connection with the cell membrane [1]. The replication origins from bacteria, phages and plasmids show few structural homologies [1].
The replication origin can be identified through its ability to confer autonomous replication to a nucleotide sequence (carrying a suitable antibiotic resistance marker) to which it is joined. In Escherichia coli, a plasmid capable of autonomous replication was constructed in this way, and the sequence acting as replication origin was identified $[2-4]$ . A similar approach in Bacillus subtilis led to the isolation of the replication origin of the defective bacteriophage PBSX [5]. The failure to isolate an autonomous replicating unit from chromosomal $B$ . subtilis DNA is attributed to the presence of two promoters in a ribosomal RNA operon; such a structure prevented the replication of the plasmid [6]. Sequences carrying replication functions were isolated from B. subtilis cryptic plasmids by joining them to appropriate selective markers [7].
In addition to the origin of replication, transcription and translation of specific plasmidic sequences have been shown to be required for replication and stability of plasmids. The regulation of colEI plasmid replication indeed involved specific RNA transcripts hybridizing with the RNA primer at the origin of replication [8]. Plasmid-coded proteins are required for replication of $E$ . coli plasmid pSC101 [9] and Staphylococcus aureus plasmid pT181 [10]. B. subtilis/Staphylococcus plasmid $\mathsf{p C}194$ codes for replication functions, as shown by sequence data and cloning experiments [11, 12]. Genetic studies with temperature-sensitive replication mutants [13] suggest the participation of a transacting gene product in the maintenance of pC194.
The aim of the present work was to clone the origin of phage 2 C by inserting in vectors viral sequences, which would allow autonomous plasmid replication in B. subtilis. When taking this approach one has to consider the uniqueness of those B. subtilis phages which have unusual bases replacing the usual DNA components (cf. [14] for review). Thus, for example, thymine is completely replaced by hydroxymethyluracil in the 100-MDa double-stranded linear DNA of phage 2C (cf. [15] for review). The semiconservative duplication of this viral DNA has been analyzed [16, 17], the sensitivity of this process to inhibitors has been explored [18], and the physical map of the 2C genome has been made. The latter studies have revealed the presence, at the extremities of 2C DNA, of large redundant ends comprising about $10\%$ of the genome [19]. These special structural components are present in all phages containing hydroxymethyluracil in their DNA; their conservation in the course of evolution is suggestive of their importance in viral economy and DNA replication [20].
This investigation is expected to contribute to the unravelling of molecular events underlying the viral cycle and the function of unusual DNA bases. In addition, comparison of the stable chimaeric plasmids, described in this work, with other B. subtilis plasmids, might help us to understand the mechanisms of replication and maintenance of plasmids within this host. It strengthens our conviction that the isolation of viral regulatory genes might provide new tools for genetic engineering in gram-positive bacteria.
## MATERIALSANDMETHODS
## Bacterial strains
B. subtilis strains $168/2~(l e u^{-},~t r p^{-})$ and SB202 (trpC2) his 2, tyr I, aro 2) were used for multiplying phage 2C, and for cloning experiments respectively. The HVC 45 strain of $E$ coli K12 (pro, leu,thr, thi, supE44 lac, tonA,hsdR-,hsd $M^{+}$ $\mathrm{Sm})$ was used for transformation experiments.
Competent cells [21] and protoplasts [22] of $B$ subtilis, and exponential cultures of $E$ 、coli [23] were used for transformation experiments with plasmid DNA. Bacteria were grown as previously described [24]. L broth, casamino (acid hydrolysate of casein) and yeast extract were from Difco Laboratories (Detroit, MI, USA).
## Preparation of phage 2C and viral DNA
Composition of growth media, and procedures for production and purification of phage 2C by buoyant density centrifugation, and for extraction and analysis of 2C DNA have been previously described [16, 24, 25].
## Selection of antibiotic-resistant transformants
Antibiotic resistance in $E$ . coli was scored by growth in the presence of ampicillin $(20\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ , tetracycline $(15\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ and chloramphenicol $(10-20\upmu\mathrm{g/ml})$ . Recombinant plasmids, expressing chloramphenicol resistance in $B$ 、subtilis，were selected by growing bacteria in the presence of 5 and $20~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}$ chloramphenicol $/\mathrm{ml}$ . Chloramphenicol, ampicillin and tetracycline were obtained from Serva (Heidelberg, FRG).
## Plasmids
Plasmid pSC540 [5, 22] was derived from the B. subtilis plasmid pC194. The latter possessed a single HpalI site, in which a 1.8-MDa HpalI fragment of $E$ coli plasmid $\mathsf{p s C101}$ was inserted (Fig.1 A); this insertion abolished its replication in $B$ . subtilis, while the Hpall fragment of pSC101 promoted pSC 540 replication in $E_{\cdot}$ coli. The chimaeric plasmid carried the chloramphenicol resistance gene of pC194, which was expressed in both $E$ coli and B. subtilis [26, 27].
pCP115 plasmid (gift from Dr C. Price, University of California, Davis) (Fig.1B) was derived from plasmid pCP112 [28] by deletion of one SalI restriction site (partial Sa/l digestion, filling by Klenow polymerase, and blunt ligation; C. Price, personal communication). pCP112 was constructed as follows. pCM133 is plasmid pBR 327 with a second SalI site at position 1429. This site was cut with AccI to give $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~C~}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~G~}}$ ends, which were ligated to the 1137-basepair HpalI-TaqI fragment of pCP194 (heavy line, Fig.1B), containing the chloramphenicol resistance gene. This plasmid was able to replicate in $E$ coli but not in B. subtilis, although the chloramphenicol resistance gene could be expressed in both hosts.
## Biochemical techniques
Restriction endonucleases were used according to instructions supplied by the manufacturer (Boehringer,
For cloning viral genes, equal amounts $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\boldmath~\mathcal~{~O~}~}}.8\ \ensuremath{\upmu}\ensuremath{\mathbf{g}}$ in $25\upmu\mathrm{l})$ of linearized plasmid and $2\mathrm{C}$ restriction fragments were submittcd to T4 ligase $(9^{\circ}\mathrm{C},16\mathrm{h})$ in the presence of $5\mathrm{mM}$ ATP.
## RESULTS
a) Cloning of phage 2 C restriction fragments in plasmids lacking a functional replication origin
As shown in Table 1, all recombinant plasmids but one lost the ability to replicate in $E$ . coli, presumably as a result of a deletion. Colonies harbouring pSC 540-derived recombinant plasmids were distinctly larger and had a grey and rough appearance, which contrasted with the normal phenotype of B. subtilis containing pSC 540, pCP115, or pCP115-1. Recombinant plasmids showed no segregational loss of antibiotic resistance and were stable (40 generations of growth in the absence of antibiotic).
## b） Size and composition of recombinant plasmids
The inability of pSC 540 recombinants to replicate in $E$ coli (Table 1) might be due to deletions occurring during the cloning procedure. To test this possibility, the molecular mass of recombinant plasmids was determined. In addition, liberation of the inserted viral DNA segment was attempted by submitting recombinant plasmids to endonuclease digestion.
As shown in column 2 of Table 2, all the recombinants of plasmid pSC 540 had similar molecular masses, ranging from $2.30\mathrm{MDa}$ to $2.60\mathrm{MDa}$ . Upon Hpall digestion all recombinant plasmids yielded similar restriction patterns. As shown in Table 2, none of the four restriction fragments had a molecular mass matching the pC194 and pSC101 moieties of plasmid pSC 540 (which derived from B.subtilis and $E.$ coli respectively). These data indicate that both plasmid moieties have undergone deletions. Moreover, release of the cloned viral fragment proved unfeasible by EcoRI hydrolysis (Table 2), a result compatible with a deletion (or alteration) of this endonuclease recognition sequence.
The molecular mass of pCP115-1 recombinant was 2.0 MDa higher than that of the original plasmid (Table 2. column 4). However, when this recombinant plasmid was submitted to EcoRl digestion, no release of the cloned viral DNA segment was obtained.
## c)Identification of viral DNA sequences inserted into recombinant plasmids
As mentioned in section (b), hydrolysis of recombinant plasmids with EcoRI, the endonuclease which had served as cloning enzyme, did not liberate the inserted segment of viral DNA. The latter was then identified by providing evidence for the presence within the recombinant plasmids of nucleotide sequences homologous to 2C DNA. In addition, cloned fragments were located within the viral genome.
Recombinant plasmid pSC 540-A was restricted with HpaI, electrophoresed, blotted on nitrocellulose by the Southern technique [33] and hybridized with different $^{32}\mathrm{P}.$ labeled probes. Lane 1 in Fig.2 shows the four Hpall fragments of the recombinant plasmid (cf. Table 2); all of them contain sequences homologous to 2C DNA (lane 3). However, by comparing lanes 1, 2 and 3 it can be inferred that the two smaller fragments of the recombinant plasmid contain only viral sequences and no sequences from the original vector.
We have previously reported that restriction fragments Hae lll D and E of 2C DNA are components of the redundant ends of the genome [19]. These sequences were separated by electrophoresis, extracted from the agarose gel, labeled by nick-translation and used as hybridization probes. It can be seen (Fig.2, lanes 4 and 5) that both viral DNA fragments hybridize with the large HpaIl fragments of the recombinant plasmid.
Three conclusions are drawn from this experiment: the two small HpaII fragments of pSC 540-A $(0.54\mathrm{MDa})$ are viral sequences, the two larger Hpall fragments contain both viral and vector sequences, and viral DNA fragments cloned in pSC 540-A partly originated from the redundant ends of the viral genome.
## d）Physical map of recombinant plasmids
In order to locate the inserted viral sequences within recombinant plasmids pSC 540-A and pCP 115-1, their physical maps were established (Fig.3) and compared with those of the original vectors pSC 540 and pCP115 [5, 22, 28] (Lannoy ct al., unpublished results). In the case of pSC 540-A, little resemblance can be seen with the native plasmid pSC 540. Deletions have occurred in both pC194 and pSC101 moieties (Table 2), which presumably led to its inability to replicate in $E$ . coli (Table 1). It thus seems probable that except for the chloramphenicol transacetylase gene, little is left of the cloning vector in the two large HpaII fragments (the two small HpaII fragments containing only viral DNA, Fig.2). The precise boundaries of viral DNA within recombinant plasmid pSC 540-A are now being determined (work in progress). On the other hand, in pCP115-1, the structure of the cloning vector was largely conserved (Fig.3), in agreement with the maintained ability of the recombinant plasmid to replicate in $E$ ： coli (Table 1) and to confer ampicillin, tetracycline and chloramphenicol resistance to this host. The increase in size $(2.2\mathrm{MDa})$ of plasmid pCP 115-1 (in comparison with cloning vector pCP115,Table 2) can thus be attributed to inserted viral sequences. This conclusion has been substantiated by Southern experiments (not shown), where the smaller restriction fragment, liberated from pCP115-1 by HindIII (see Fig.3, bottom: dashed line), hybridized to 2C DNA.
e）Localization of cloned 2 C-DNA fragment on the physical map of the viral genome
Previous work from our laboratory has established the physical map of phage 2C chromosome and identified large redundancies at the extremities of the molecule [19, 20]. It was of interest to locate the viral DNA segments of recombinant plasmids with respect to the map of 2C DNA. Accordingly, recombinant plasmids were labeled by nick-translation and hybridized by the Southern technique with the Sall-Hae IlI, BglII and EcoRI restriction fragments of 2C DNA. The hybridization patterns thus obtained are displayed in Fig.4.
It can be seen that all recombinant plasmids, derived from both pSC540 and pCP115, yield similar hybridization patterns and hybridize to multiple viral fragments. Indeed, labeled recombinant plasmids hybridize to 2C DNA Sa/lHae IIl fragments A and C (or D), Bg/II fragments A, C and K (Fig.4) and fragments EcoRI D, Q and H (not shown). Interestingly, these viral segments contain repetitive sequences [19] (Fig.4 and unpublished results). This is shown by comparing the hybridization patterns of the recombinant plasmids with those obtained with labeled 2C HaeIlI D (Fig.4A, lane 6) and SalI C (Fig.4B, lane 4). This latter fragment was previously shown to contain one whole repetitive end [19]. In addition, three out of the thirteen recombinant plasmids, which were analyzed, hybridized also to fragments EcoRI L and Bg/II J (Fig.4), which are not part of the redundant end [19] (and unpublished results). The exact location of these fragments is not known, and no ordering of the Bg/II restriction fragments of 2C DNA is yet available.
It can be concluded that the inserted viral segments of recombinant plasmids largely, but not exclusively, originate from the redundant extremities of 2C DNA. This conclusion is in agreement with the results reported in section (c) and applies to all thirteen recombinant plasmids.
## DISCUSSION
Experiments described in the section (a) of the Results describe the obtaining of 13 recombinant plasmids, whereby the insertion of viral segments was correlated with the acquired ability of these plasmids to replicate in B. subtilis, as shown by the expression of chloramphenicol resistance in this host. The fact that all recombinant plasmids hybridized to the same 2C DNA restriction segments (Fig.4) suggests that these segments are responsible for the autonomous replication of the recombinant plasmids.
According to Horinouchi and Weisblum [11], the integrity of the unique MspI (HpaII) site of $\mathsf{p C}194$ is essential for its replication in B. subtilis, since an insertion into this site of a pSC101 fragment ( $E$ 、 coli plasmid) abolishes replication of the recombinant pSC 540 in B. subtilis [22]. Although in other experiments [12] insertion of pBR 322 DNA in the same HpaII site of pC 194 did not entail a loss of autonomous replication, both the transforming activity and the copy number of this hybrid plasmid were decreased. These experiments can hardly be compared to those reported in the present work. In fact, it is unlikely that the acquisition of autonomous replication of pSC 540 recombinants (Table 2 and Fig.3) is due to a restoration of the original plasmid sequences around this HpaIl site;
indeed the EcoRI cloning site (which was used for construction of our pSC540 recombinants) is located far from the HpaII site (Fig.1A). The alternative interpretation of our data is that the cloned viral sequences are indeed responsible for plasmid replication in B. subtilis.
Cloning vector pCP115, which is equivalent to plasmid pHW9 [11], carries the chloramphenicol transacetylase gene of pC194 and part of reading frame C (it lacks reading frames A and D). From the available data [11, 12], it is impossible to know if and to what extent any pC194 sequence involved in replication was conserved within the TaqI-HpaII fragment inserted in pCP 115 (Fig.1 B). At this stage we assume that the cloned viral replication sequences have allowed replication of the recombinant plasmids. This conclusion is further supported by the obtention, after insertion of 2C DNA restriction fragments into pHV32, of one B. subtilis Cm recombinant (M. Coene and E. Nyssen, personal communication). This cloning vector (S. D.Ehrlich, CRBM, Paris VII), which was obtained from pHV 33 by deletion, was able to replicate in $E$ coli but not in B. subtilis. It has been used previously to clone the replication origins of some cryptic B. subtilis plasmids [7]
Data in Figs 2 and 4 indicate that the viral DNA segments cloned in pSC 540-derived and pCP 115-derived plasmide have originated from the redundant ends, and possibly from the adjacent portions, of phage 2C genome. This conclusion agrees with pulse-labeling experiments showing that replication of DNA of the related phage SPo1 starts within, or close to, the terminal redundancies, at about $8\%$ from the chromosome ends [34]. The cloned 2C DNA segments, allowing autonomous replication of all the recombinant plasmids, originated from the same region of the phage genome (Fig.4). This is yet an additional argument in favor of the cloned fragments carrying the viral replication functions.
Restriction mapping (Fig.3) and hybridization experiments (Figs 2 and 4) have provided consistent evidence for the presence of large fragments of viral DNA in recombinant plasmids (more than $0.5\mathrm{MDa}$ in pSC 540-A and $2.0\:\mathrm{MDa}$ in pCP115-1). The functions encoded by these cloned segments are unknown. Note that the members of the hydroxymethyluracil-containing DNA family code for their own DNA polymerase [35], as well as for enzymes involved in the biosynthesis of the abnormal base [14]. A possible involvement of the cloned viral DNA segments in these functions would be of great interest; such an approach is presently considered.
We have previously reported that 2C DNA is refractory to PstI cleavage [19]. Our present data (Table 4 and Fig.3) show the occurrence of two PstI sites within the viral segment inserted into pCP 115-1. This apparent discrepancy might bc due to the fact that during replication of the recombinant plasmid, the abnormal base, hydroxymethyluracil, is replaced by thymine. It must be recalled, in fact, that B. subtilis misses the virus-specific metabolic pathway leading to the synthesis of this abnormal precursor in 2C-infected bacteria (cf. [15] for review).
